 **CI/CD approach**
-- GitHub Actions, three workflows. `ci.yml`, triggered on push and pull request, with matrix `os: [windows-2025, macos-latest, ubuntu-latest]` on native runners, is the only push/PR pipeline. `nightly.yml`, triggered by a weekly `schedule` and `workflow_dispatch` (both fire from the repository's default branch), runs three ubuntu jobs with no cache. `advisories` runs `cargo deny check advisories` over the root lockfile and `cargo deny --manifest-path fuzz/Cargo.toml check advisories` over the fuzz lockfile, because the advisory DB moves without code changes; `npm-advisories` does the same for `e2e-web/package-lock.json` (`Node (pinned)`, then `scripts/npm-audit.sh --advisories-only`). `fuzz` runs `cargo +<channel> fuzz run --fuzz-dir fuzz <t> fuzz/corpus/<t> -- -max_total_time=120` per target (the channel comes from `fuzz/rust-toolchain.toml`) and uploads `fuzz/artifacts/` on `failure()`. `windows-mutants.yml` (founder ruling C2, 2026-10-04) is triggered by `workflow_dispatch` only, with no `inputs:`, and is dispatched only during the epoch-boundary audit. It is never a gate, never a required check and never a dependency of `ci.yml`, and it adds no check to a push run. One dispatch was made outside that audit, on 2026-10-10, for chunk 2026-10-10-windows-mutation-grade alone and on the founder's own word for it (run 38036448183); the ruling stands as written. One job definition, named `mutants (<label>)`, runs on `windows-2025` with `contents: read`, `timeout-minutes: 120` and `fail-fast: false` over a ten-item `matrix.include` (counted as its `- package:` items; each item is a `package`, a `label` and Windows-gated `files`). The root package `viola` is five of the ten, split by file (`src/cmd/run.rs`; `src/main.rs` with `src/conpty.rs`; `src/run/env.rs`; `src/panic_frames.rs`; `src/cmd/verify/stop.rs`, label `viola-cmd-verify-stop`, added at chunk 2026-10-13-interrupted-verify-cleanup because the contract test below requires every product file with a `cfg(windows)` gate to be listed; nothing was dispatched), so that each job ends inside the ceiling. `tests/contract_windows_mutation_scope.rs` keeps the file lists equal to the gated sources, the workflow's tool pins equal to the `test` job's, and the labels distinct, one per item. Its steps are checkout, `rustup toolchain install` and install-action with `cargo-nextest@0.9.146,cargo-mutants@27.1.0`, then one `shell: pwsh` step that reads `PACKAGE` / `FILES` from step `env:` and runs `scripts/agent-run.ps1 run --mutants --package <member> --file …`. It has no cache, upload, secret, `concurrency:` or `needs:`. Its job log, the harness document plus cargo-mutants' outcome lines, is the audit's read, and the harness document judges a job. The harness leaves a missed mutant the Windows build never compiled (a `#[cfg(unix)]` twin in a scoped file) out of the count and names it in the document's `mutants.host_excluded` (test-plan §3 `run` step 4), while cargo-mutants' own lines still say MISSED for it. Run 38036448183 read all nine jobs green with 30 mutants left out, as measured at `viola-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-10-windows-mutation-grade/evidence/windows-dispatch.md`. No workflow has a `concurrency:` block. zizmor's pedantic `concurrency-limits` was declined because a concurrency group cancels pending runs, which would drop a cancelled run's `always()` gate and upload chain. (No push or pull-request run carries a mutation job — mutation testing moved to the epoch-boundary code audit on 2026-09-28, and `windows-mutants.yml` uploads nothing — so a cancelled run cannot lose mutation coverage; that half of the original reason is retired.)
